87f5937e-5a88-4caf-882d-bfe4a739434f	a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013	\N	0	0	tanvijoship0_0	\N	draft	Month 1 - Build the foundation. The goal here is not perfection but consistency: you're installing habits, not chasing dramatic results. Keep meals simple and repeatable so you don't have to think hard. A typical day might open with a high-protein breakfast such as three eggs or a bowl of Greek yogurt with berries and oats, giving you 30-40 grams of protein to start the day full. Lunch centres on a palm-to-two-palms portion of lean protein - grilled chicken, turkey, tofu, fish, or lentils - piled next to a large volume of vegetables and a fist-sized serving of a slow carbohydrate like brown rice, quinoa, or sweet potato. An afternoon snack of fruit with a handful of nuts, or cottage cheese, bridges you to dinner, which mirrors lunch: another lean protein, plenty of vegetables cooked in a little olive oil, and a smaller carb portion since the day is winding down. Through this first month, focus on eating protein at every meal, getting vegetables into at least two meals a day, and cutting the easiest wins - sugary drinks, most alcohol, and mindless snacking - rather than banning entire food groups. Allow yourself one relaxed meal each week so the plan feels livable.\n\nMonth 2 - Refine and tighten. By now the habits are automatic, so this month is about sharpening the details and responding to your body. As you lose weight your maintenance calories drift down slightly, so if the scale has stalled for two weeks, trim another 100-150 calories, usually from carbs or fats rather than protein. Tighten portion awareness - this is where casually eyeballing servings often hides a few hundred extra calories - and lean harder on high-volume, low-calorie foods that let you eat a lot while staying in a deficit: leafy greens, cucumbers, peppers, tomatoes, berries, broth-based soups, and lean proteins. A representative day might be an omelette with spinach and mushrooms plus a piece of fruit in the morning; a big chicken-and-quinoa salad loaded with vegetables and a light dressing at lunch; a protein shake or a small tin of tuna mid-afternoon; and a dinner of baked salmon or lean beef with roasted vegetables and a modest portion of potatoes. Keep the weekly free meal, but treat it as a single meal, not a whole day, so it doesn't erase the week's deficit. Adding daily walking (aiming for something like 8,000-10,000 steps) quietly widens your deficit without extra hunger.\n\nMonth 3 - Push through and lock it in. The final month is about breaking any plateau and preparing for the transition to maintenance so the fat stays off. Plateaus are normal, and the fix is usually one of three levers: a small further calorie reduction, a genuine increase in daily movement, or a tighter look at whether tracking has quietly drifted. To keep hormones and adherence healthy during a longer diet, you can use an occasional "refeed" - a day or two at maintenance calories with the extra coming mostly from carbohydrates - which can restore energy and training performance without derailing progress. Meals stay in the same mould you've now mastered: protein-forward, vegetable-heavy, with carbohydrates timed around your most active parts of the day. A sample day could be Greek yogurt with berries and a scoop of protein at breakfast; a stir-fry of shrimp or chicken with a rainbow of vegetables and a modest scoop of rice at lunch; an apple with peanut butter or some edamame in the afternoon; and a large mixed-vegetable and lean-protein bowl for dinner. As you approach your goal, begin nudging calories back up gradually toward your new, lower maintenance level rather than snapping back to old habits, which is what prevents the classic post-diet rebound.\n\nA flexible weekly rhythm. Rather than eating thirty different things, build a rotation of perhaps four or five breakfasts, five or six lunches and dinners, and a handful of snacks, then repeat them - repetition is what makes a diet stick. Cook proteins and grains in batches at the start of the week, keep frozen vegetables and pre-cooked protein on hand for busy evenings, and always have a fast default meal (like eggs and vegetables, or yogurt and fruit) so hunger never forces you into a poor choice. Lean toward whole, minimally processed foods most of the time - lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, legumes, whole grains, fruits, vegetables, nuts, and healthy oils - and keep highly processed, calorie-dense, low-satiety foods to the margins rather than the centre of your eating.		{}	admin@	2026-08-16 15:49:21.372044+05:30	2026-08-16 15:49:23.78103+05:30	\N	1	0
